{"at":"2026-11-16T12:33:30.365604+00:00","done_chunks":["Approval voting|53145ffdaccd","Approval voting|c50574622986","Electoral reform in the United States|98b06ecb6716","Instant-runoff voting|18e666b8c66c","Instant-runoff voting|48b434267704","Instant-runoff voting|91bb4419bf25","Instant-runoff voting|adde6c69d38d","Instant-runoff voting|d16b6362775e","Instant-runoff voting|df335059fb6c","Instant-runoff voting|e7afdba60baf","Ranked-choice voting in the United States|f51830c98783","STAR voting|69de438db473"],"items":[{"detail":"Voters approved Measure 2 to establish a nonpartisan top-four primary with an instant-runoff general election for all state, federal, and presidential general elections.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"AK","status":"adopted","system":"Top-four/Final-five + RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2020"},{"detail":"A measure seeking to repeal the ranked-choice voting system gathered enough signatures to qualify for the 2026 ballot as 2026 Alaska Measure 2.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"AK","status":"ballot measure","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2026"},{"detail":"Alaska uses ranked-choice voting to require a majority of votes for a candidate to receive electoral votes in presidential elections.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"AK","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electoral_reform_in_the_United_States","year":""},{"detail":"Alaska's at-large congressional district special election in 2022 used instant-runoff voting, where the Republican candidate Sarah Palin lost and the Democrat Mary Peltola won.","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free","party":"","scope":"congressional","source":"wikipedia","state":"AK","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":"2022"},{"detail":"Instant-runoff voting is used for general elections after a top-four nonpartisan primary.","model":"inclusionai/ling-3.2-flash:free","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"AK","status":"in use","system":"Top-four/Final-five + RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":""},{"detail":"A ballot measure to repeal the ranked-choice voting system and return to partisan primaries narrowly failed by 737 votes.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"AK","status":"rejected","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2024"},{"detail":"State law uses 'instant runoff' to describe certain absentee voters casting ranked-choice ballots before the first round, counted in any subsequent runoff election.","model":"inclusionai/ling-3.2-flash:free","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"AR","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":""},{"detail":"Approval voting is used in internal elections by the Libertarian Party of Arizona.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"Libertarian Party of Arizona","scope":"party","source":"wikipedia","state":"AZ","status":"in use","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Approval_voting","year":""},{"detail":"California cities Oakland, San Francisco, and San Leandro used instant-runoff voting in 2010 elections where the IRV winner was the Condorcet winner in four elections.","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free","party":"","scope":"local","source":"wikipedia","state":"CA","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":"2010"},{"detail":"Approval voting is used in internal elections by the Libertarian Party of Colorado.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"Libertarian Party of Colorado","scope":"party","source":"wikipedia","state":"CO","status":"in use","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Approval_voting","year":""},{"detail":"The Colorado legislature considered approval voting in 2017, but the bill was postponed indefinitely.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"CO","status":"proposed","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electoral_reform_in_the_United_States","year":"2017"},{"detail":"Voters passed Initiative 83 to allow independents to vote in party primaries and implement ranked-choice voting across all presidential, federal, and district elections by 2026.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"DC","status":"adopted","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2024"},{"detail":"Senate Bill 2162 was enacted allowing instant-runoff voting to be used in special elections for federal and county council offices.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"","scope":"congressional","source":"wikipedia","state":"HI","status":"adopted","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2022"},{"detail":"Springfield, Illinois allows absentee voters to submit ranked ballots in runoff elections so their votes can count in the second round if their first choice is eliminated.","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free","party":"","scope":"local","source":"wikipedia","state":"IL","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":""},{"detail":"Louisiana allows ranked ballots for absentee voters who are members of the United States Service or reside overseas in runoff elections.","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"LA","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":""},{"detail":"The Maine Legislature passed a bill adopting instant-runoff voting for presidential primaries, which Governor Janet Mills allowed to become law without her signature.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"","scope":"presidential primary","source":"wikipedia","state":"ME","status":"adopted","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2019"},{"detail":"Instant-runoff voting is used for general elections and for primary elections held separately by each party, excluding elections for president.","model":"inclusionai/ling-3.2-flash:free","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"ME","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":""},{"detail":"Instant-runoff voting was retained for the 2020 U.S. Senate and U.S. House elections after being used in the 2nd congressional district election.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"","scope":"congressional","source":"wikipedia","state":"ME","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2020"},{"detail":"The Maine Republican Party gathered signatures for a veto referendum to repeal instant-runoff voting for the 2020 presidential election, but it failed to qualify for the ballot.","model":"gemini-3.8-flash","party":"Republican","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"ME","status":"petition drive","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranked-choice_voting_in_the_United_States","year":"2020"},{"detail":"Minneapolis uses instant-runoff voting and has used the term ranked-choice voting in its laws.","model":"inclusionai/ling-3.2-flash:free","party":"","scope":"local","source":"wikipedia","state":"MN","status":"in use","system":"RCV","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant-runoff_voting","year":""},{"detail":"St. Louis passed Proposition D with 70% support to authorize a variant of approval voting for municipal offices.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"local","source":"wikipedia","state":"MO","status":"adopted","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Approval_voting","year":"2020"},{"detail":"St. Louis passed Proposition D in November 2020 to authorize a variant of approval voting for municipal offices.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"local","source":"wikipedia","state":"MO","status":"in use","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electoral_reform_in_the_United_States","year":"2020"},{"detail":"Fargo, North Dakota, passed a local ballot initiative with 63% support adopting approval voting for the city's local elections.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"local","source":"wikipedia","state":"ND","status":"adopted","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Approval_voting","year":"2018"},{"detail":"Governor Kelly Armstrong signed a bill banning ranked-choice voting and approval voting in the state, ending the practice in Fargo.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"ND","status":"banned","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Approval_voting","year":"2025"},{"detail":"Fargo, North Dakota, passed a local ballot initiative in 2018 adopting approval voting for the city's local elections.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"local","source":"wikipedia","state":"ND","status":"in use","system":"Approval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electoral_reform_in_the_United_States","year":"2018"},{"detail":"The North Dakota Legislative Assembly introduced Bill HB 1273 in 2023 to ban approval voting statewide, which was vetoed by the governor and the veto was not overridden in the senate.","model":"qwen/qwen3.8-27b","party":"","scope":"statewide","source":"wikipedia","state":"ND","status":"proposed","syst